Subject: The unit can hold a shape and ask where a property is, without marking the shape observed

What this does
  - `qjs_aot_shape_rslot` / `qjs_aot_shape_wslot`: the byte offset of an own data property for a read / for a store (also writable, not the array length), without marking the shape observed.
  - `qjs_aot_shape_hold` / `qjs_aot_shape_release`: a counted reference to a shape, and its release.
  - `qjs_aot_function_digest`: the digest of a bytecode function (the unit's binding key) whether or not it has been called, so a dispatch can tell which compiled function a lookup found before it first runs.

Why we need it
  The new backend's fast bodies store at a constant slot after verifying a receiver shape once, and keep the verified shapes in a table keyed by address. Holding a counted reference means the shape can be neither freed (its address cannot be reused) nor edited in place (an edit of a shape somebody else references clones it first), so the table needs no epoch. The read form `qjs_aot_shape_slot` marks the shape observed, and then every free or in-place edit of it moves shape_obs_epoch, which the other compiled tables validate: observing the instance shapes of a program that creates and frees them constantly flushed them (deltablue: C.Minstr doubled).

Soundness
  Reads the shape only. A held shape stays valid and unchanged for the life of the reference; the unit releases its references at teardown, before the runtime is freed.

Functions touched
  `qjs_aot_shape_rslot`, `qjs_aot_shape_wslot`, `qjs_aot_shape_hold`, `qjs_aot_shape_release`, `qjs_aot_function_digest` (new).

No.

